@page "/events/tutorials/06-scoped-state"

<TutorialDetail Tutorials="TutorialSet.Events" Slug="06-scoped-state">
<p><strong>Goal:</strong> put the same widget on a page several times, each with its <strong>own</strong> copy of a state type, while code inside the widget stays as simple as with global state.</p>

<p><em>See it running:</em> the <strong>Scoped widgets</strong> sample page (<a href="/events/example/scoped-widgets"><code>/events/example/scoped-widgets</code></a>) and the right half of <strong>Plain components</strong>.</p>

<h2 id="the-idea">The idea</h2>

<p>By default every state type has one instance, at the root. <code>&lt;StateScope TState="X"&gt;</code> creates a new instance of <code>X</code> for its subtree. Inside the subtree, anything that asks for <code>X</code> (<code>[UseState]</code>, <code>State&lt;X&gt;()</code>) gets the <strong>nearest</strong> instance:</p>

<pre><code>Root                              PollState #root
├── PollCard "Lunch"
│   └── StateScope&lt;PollState&gt;     PollState #1  ← everything inside resolves here
└── PollCard "Offsite"
    └── StateScope&lt;PollState&gt;     PollState #2</code></pre>

<p>Components inside a poll don't know or care which instance they get. That's the point.</p>

<h2 id="1-define-the-state">1. Define the state</h2>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/PollState.cs
using System.Collections.Immutable;

namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Polls;

public sealed record PollState(string Question, ImmutableDictionary&lt;string, int&gt; Votes) : IState&lt;PollState&gt;
{
    public static PollState Initial =&gt; new("Untitled poll", ImmutableDictionary&lt;string, int&gt;.Empty);

    public static PollState Create(string question, params string[] options) =&gt;
        new(question, options.ToImmutableDictionary(o =&gt; o, _ =&gt; 0));

    public int TotalVotes =&gt; Votes.Values.Sum();

    public PollState Vote(string option) =&gt; this with { Votes = Votes.SetItem(option, Votes[option] + 1) };
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="2-wrap-the-widget-in-a-statescope">2. Wrap the widget in a <code>StateScope</code></h2>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/Components/PollCard.razor *@@
&lt;StateScope TState="PollState" Initial="Poll"&gt;
    &lt;div class="card mb-3"&gt;
        &lt;div class="card-header"&gt;&lt;PollQuestion /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div class="card-body"&gt;&lt;PollOptions /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div class="card-footer"&gt;&lt;PollTotal /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/StateScope&gt;

@@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public PollState Poll { get; set; } = default!;
}</code></pre>

<p><code>Initial</code> is optional. Without it, the scope starts from <code>PollState.Initial</code>. It's <strong>read once</strong>: changing the parameter later doesn't reset the poll. Write to the store instead.</p>

<h2 id="3-write-the-children-as-if-the-state-were-global">3. Write the children as if the state were global</h2>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/Components/PollQuestion.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;strong&gt;@@Question&lt;/strong&gt;</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/Components/PollQuestion.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Polls.Components;

public partial class PollQuestion
{
    [UseState(Select = nameof(SelectQuestion))]
    private partial string Question { get; }

    private static string SelectQuestion(PollState poll) =&gt; poll.Question;
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/Components/PollOptions.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

@@foreach (var (option, votes) in Poll.Votes.OrderBy(v =&gt; v.Key))
{
    &lt;button class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm me-2" @@onclick="() =&gt; Vote(option)"&gt;@@option (@@votes)&lt;/button&gt;
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/Components/PollOptions.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Polls.Components;

public partial class PollOptions
{
    [UseState]
    private partial PollState Poll { get; }

    // Resolves to this card's PollState, not the root's and not another card's.
    private void Vote(string option) =&gt; State&lt;PollState&gt;().Update(p =&gt; p.Vote(option));
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/Components/PollTotal.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;small&gt;@@Total vote(s)&lt;/small&gt;</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/Components/PollTotal.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Polls.Components;

public partial class PollTotal
{
    [UseState(Select = nameof(SelectTotal))]
    private partial int Total { get; }

    private static int SelectTotal(PollState poll) =&gt; poll.TotalVotes;
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="4-use-it-several-times">4. Use it several times</h2>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Pages/PollsPage.razor *@@
@@page "/polls"
@@rendermode InteractiveAuto
@@using MyApp.Client.Features.Polls
@@using MyApp.Client.Features.Polls.Components

&lt;PollCard Poll="@@PollState.Create("Lunch?", "Pizza", "Sushi", "Salad")" /&gt;
&lt;PollCard Poll="@@PollState.Create("Offsite?", "Beach", "Mountains")" /&gt;</code></pre>

<p>Each card counts its own votes.</p>

<h2 id="5-reach-past-the-scope">5. Reach past the scope</h2>

<p>Sometimes a component inside a scope needs the <strong>root</strong> instance of the same type. Point resolution at the root:</p>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/Components/FeaturedPollBadge.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Polls.Components;

/// &lt;summary&gt;Placed inside a PollCard, but shows the app-wide featured poll held at the root.&lt;/summary&gt;
public partial class FeaturedPollBadge
{
    [UseState(Select = nameof(SelectQuestion), Scope = ScopeKind.Root)]
    private partial string FeaturedQuestion { get; }

    [UseState(Select = nameof(SelectQuestion))]
    private partial string ThisQuestion { get; }

    private static string SelectQuestion(PollState poll) =&gt; poll.Question;
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/Components/FeaturedPollBadge.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

@@if (FeaturedQuestion == ThisQuestion)
{
    &lt;span class="badge text-bg-warning"&gt;Featured&lt;/span&gt;
}</code></pre>

<p>Types that <strong>no</strong> <code>StateScope</code> provides always fall back to the root, so you only need <code>ScopeKind.Root</code> when the same type exists at both levels. <code>ScopeName = "…"</code> works too, to start resolution at a named scope.</p>

<h2 id="6-scopes-events-and-cleanup">6. Scopes, events and cleanup</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>A <code>StateScope</code> is also an event scope.</strong> For components inside it, it's their nearest scope: <code>Route.Local</code> from inside reaches only that scope's listeners, and <code>[Subscribe]</code> handlers listen there by default. Give it a <code>Name</code> to target it with <code>Route.Named</code> (<a href="/events/tutorials/05-scopes-and-routing">tutorial 05</a>).</li>
<li><strong>To provide several state types</strong> to one subtree, nest <code>StateScope</code>s.</li>
<li><strong>When the <code>StateScope</code> leaves the page</strong> (e.g. the card is removed), its store is disposed along with all subscriptions to it.</li>
</ul>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/Components/PollCardWithNotes.razor *@@
@@* Two scoped state types for one widget: nest the scopes. *@@
&lt;StateScope TState="PollState" Name="poll" Initial="Poll"&gt;
    &lt;StateScope TState="PollNotesState"&gt;
        &lt;PollQuestion /&gt;
        &lt;PollOptions /&gt;
    &lt;/StateScope&gt;
&lt;/StateScope&gt;

@@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public PollState Poll { get; set; } = default!;
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Polls/PollNotesState.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Polls;

public sealed record PollNotesState(string Text) : IState&lt;PollNotesState&gt;
{
    public static PollNotesState Initial =&gt; new("");
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>

<ul>
<li><code>StateScope</code> creates a child scope, registers it as the <strong>provider</strong> of <code>TState</code>, and cascades the scope (fixed, so the cascade costs nothing).</li>
<li>Resolution walks up from the component's nearest scope to the first provider of the type. Root provides every type implicitly.</li>
<li>Each component resolves once, at initialization, so the lookup isn't repeated on every render.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="-best-practices">Best practices</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Put the <code>StateScope</code> in the widget's root component</strong> (<code>PollCard</code>), so callers just write <code>&lt;PollCard /&gt;</code> and can't forget it.</li>
<li><strong>Pass starting data through <code>Initial</code></strong> from the widget's parameters. Don't write to the store from the parent.</li>
<li><strong>Write child components as if the state were global.</strong> If a child needs to know it's scoped, the design is usually leaking.</li>
<li><strong>Use selectors in scoped widgets too.</strong> A list of 50 widgets that each re-render on every change adds up.</li>
<li><strong>Nest scopes for multiple state types</strong>, outermost first, so the structure reads top-down.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>

<div class="table-container"><table class="table is-striped">
<thead>
<tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>All cards share one value</td><td><code>StateScope</code> missing, so everything resolves to the root</td><td>Wrap the widget in <code>&lt;StateScope TState="…"&gt;</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Changing <code>Initial</code> does nothing</td><td>It's read once at initialization (a warning is logged)</td><td>Write to the store</td></tr>
<tr><td>A component in the card sees the root value</td><td>It sits <em>outside</em> the <code>StateScope</code> element</td><td>Move it inside</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Route.Local</code> from inside the card misses a listener outside the <code>StateScope</code></td><td>The <code>StateScope</code> is the nearest scope</td><td>Use <code>Route.Bubble</code> or <code>Route.Named</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

